    "durationMs": 184842,
    "finalText": "Done. The URL Shortener API is complete and fully verified.\n\n**What I built** (empty workspace, so created the project from scratch):\n- `Program.cs` — minimal API: `POST /links` (201 with `{code, url, clicks:0}`, 409 on duplicate code, 422 on malformed JSON / bad url / bad code via manual JSON parsing), `GET /links` (ascending order, `{items, total}`), `GET /links/{code}` (200/404), `GET /r/{code}` (302 + Location, atomic `ExecuteUpdate` increment so clicks count exactly once even under concurrency)\n- `AppDbContext.cs` / `Link.cs` / `Migrations/` — EF Core 8 + Npgsql with a unique index on `code`, migrations generated via a local dotnet-ef tool manifest\n- `DatabaseConfig.cs` — parses the `DATABASE_URL` PostgreSQL URL (credentials only from env, nothing hard-coded)\n- `start.sh` — applies pending migrations, then serves in the foreground on `0.0.0.0:8000`\n- `README.md` — start-the-server section plus endpoint docs\n\n**Verification**: a 35-check curl suite passed 35/35 covering every requirement (generated codes 4–20 chars, duplicate → 409, all 422 cases, 404s, 302 Location exactness, list ordering). I then restarted the server via `sh start.sh` — state was byte-identical after restart (clicks and links persisted in Postgres) and redirects kept incrementing. Test server has been stopped, so port 8000 is free."
